### The Test Cabinet ###

# Ignore cspell's autogenerated cache file
.cspellcache

# Rendered reference screenshots are a build output: the harness renders each
# HTML mockup to a screenshot under the case's `reference/.rendered/` cache (see
# crates/core/src/reference.rs). That cache is regenerated and must never be
# committed — for ANY case, any type/difficulty/slug/version.
test-cases/**/reference/.rendered/

# `reference/screenshots/`, by contrast, is NOT ignored: it holds the *committed*
# media screenshots a `media`-based case serves as-is (captured from its playable
# reference-impl — the source of truth, since it has no mockup to render). Because
# the rendered cache lives elsewhere, these can be tracked wholesale with no
# per-case enumeration, so a new media-based case's screenshots are committed
# automatically.

# Cargo build output. Cargo.lock is intentionally committed because this
# workspace builds applications (the CLI and desktop app), not just libraries.
/target/
**/*.rs.bk

# gg's eleven program-language signature catalogues, which are BUILD ARTIFACTS.
# `crates/gg/build.rs` reflects each one out of that arm's own SDK, with that
# arm's own documentation tool, into the build's `OUT_DIR` — so a catalogue never
# lands in the source tree on the path that matters, and `/target/` above already
# covers it.
#
# Both rules below are therefore about the paths a PERSON produces, and they are
# deliberately unlike each other:
#
#   * `scripts/gg-signatures.sh` writes into `target/gg-signatures/` by default,
#     because reading a catalogue is how a reflector bug is found (a dropped
#     `@return` paragraph, a truncated parameter description, only the first
#     overload's prose) and a person has to be able to open one. Covered by
#     `/target/` already; named here so the directory is not mistaken for
#     something that escaped the rule.
#   * `scripts/gg-artifacts.sh` is the same shape one level up and needs no rule
#     here: it defaults to `target/gg-artifacts/` and REFUSES to run without a
#     destination when called per-arm, so no arrangement of `git add` can commit
#     what it writes.
/target/gg-signatures/
/target/gg-artifacts/

# THE TWO DIRECTORIES THE ARTIFACTS USED TO BE COMMITTED IN, as an ALLOWLIST.
#
# `crates/gg/src/sandbox/guests/` held the baked `.wasm` guest components and,
# before them, the signature catalogues; `checkers/` beside it held the compiled
# library sets, the archives, the adapters and the compilers. Thirty-three files,
# ~117 MB. Every one of them is cut into a cargo `OUT_DIR` now, by a crate under
# `crates/gg-sandbox-artifacts/`, on the same build that compiles the host that
# embeds it. `guests/` does not exist at all any more.
#
# WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY IT IS A GITIGNORE RATHER THAN A TEST. Two rules used
# to enforce the completeness of that decision, and both were deleted with the
# artifacts they were written for: a test that walked both directories and failed
# on any file not argued for by name, and a loop in `scripts/ci/contract-drift.sh`
# that did the same over `checkers/*`. They were the right shape — "every file gg
# embeds from must be ARGUED" — and losing them left `check-added-large-files` at
# its 500 kB default as the only thing standing over these paths, which would have
# admitted `cpp.adapter.wasm` (52 kB), `typescript.checker.json`, every
# `*.toolchain.json` and both `*.compiler.json` back into git without a word.
#
# An allowlist is the same rule with nothing to run: what is left under `checkers/`
# is gg's OWN HAND-WRITTEN SOURCE — three Java files that are the compiler drivers
# gg feeds to `javac`, and the two JVM pin declarations — so those five are named
# and everything else in the directory is ignored. Adding a sixth file there is
# then a deliberate line in THIS file, which is a place a reviewer will ask what
# the file is; adding one without the line is a file `git add` silently declines.
# `guests/` is ignored whole, because there is nothing left that belongs in it.
crates/gg/src/sandbox/checkers/*
!crates/gg/src/sandbox/checkers/java.compiler.java
!crates/gg/src/sandbox/checkers/java.toolchain.json
!crates/gg/src/sandbox/checkers/jvm.backend.java
!crates/gg/src/sandbox/checkers/kotlin.compiler.java
!crates/gg/src/sandbox/checkers/kotlin.toolchain.json
crates/gg/src/sandbox/guests/

# Ignore the output of test runs. Anchored to the repository root: `tcab` writes
# runs to ./runs (see `--out-dir`), but an unanchored `runs/` also matches source
# directories such as apps/site/src/pages/runs/, silently excluding real code.
/runs/

# Scratch output for inspecting seeded run inputs (`tcab seed`). This is local
# inspection material, not something that should be committed.
tmp/

# Ignore generated workflows
.claude/workflows/

# Ignore Sqlite files used by services
tcab-auth.sqlite*
tcab-backend.sqlite*

# The backend's on-disk definition store (default TCAB_BACKEND_STORE=./tcab-store).
# Generated by ingest from the checkout — local state, not source. Anchored to the
# repo root so it can't match a nested source directory of the same name.
/tcab-store/

# Ignore driver-generated run scratch + the local artifact-service store
/.tcab-driver/
/tcab-artifacts/

# `tcab validate --implementation <dir>` writes synthesized validation media into a
# `.vendor/` folder inside the target build directory (e.g. a case's reference-impl).
# It is transient output, regenerated on demand — never source. Not anchored, so it
# matches the folder at any depth.
.vendor/

# scripts/reingest.sh change-detection baseline (its mtime = last successful
# re-ingest). Per-checkout local state, not source. Anchored to the repo root.
/.reingest-timestamp

# Claude memories — host-mounted for container-rebuild persistence, not committed
.claude/memories/

# Python bytecode caches (e.g. from the Blender container's helper modules being
# imported during local development — see containers/blender/). Never source.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd
.pytest_cache/

# Compiled class files, for the same reason — the JVM arms build their SDK into a
# jar the build cuts into an `OUT_DIR` (it used to be tracked at
# `crates/gg/src/sandbox/checkers/*.sdk.jar`, and is not any more), and a loose
# `.class` beside its source is always a local build leaving litter.
*.class

# Note for anyone tempted to widen this into "ignore binary files": do not. This
# repository still commits binaries on purpose — the Foray reference guests'
# `.wasm`, the reference implementations' `.mid` music, the desktop app's
# `.icns`. What belongs here is COMPILED SOURCE, which is derived from something
# else in the tree and regenerated by building it; a committed artifact that
# nothing in the tree can rebuild is not that. (gg's eleven arms used to be the
# largest counterexample on the other side — 33 blobs, ~117 MB of guest
# components, SDK jars and library archives. They are all generated now, which is
# why this note names fewer things than it once did.)

# Local reference-sprite preview renders: the asset cases' reference draw scripts
# (reference-impl/base/draw.sh) rendered to per-frame PNGs/GIFs by
# scripts/preview-asset-reference.mjs, for eyeballing sprites while iterating.
# Regenerable output, not source — kept on disk to review, never committed.
/reference-previews/
